    "text": "To find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity, we start by analyzing the given polynomial.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the polynomial:\n\\[\nz^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can make a substitution to simplify the equation. Let \\( w = z^2 \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\nw^2 + w + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\( w \\). We solve it using the quadratic formula \\( w = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 1 \\), and \\( c = 1 \\):\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\( w \\) are:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad w = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\( w = z^2 \\). Therefore, we need to find \\( z \\) such that:\n\\[\nz^2 = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z^2 = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThese values are complex numbers on the unit circle in the complex plane. We recognize that:\n\\[\n\\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} = e^{i\\pi/3} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} = e^{-i\\pi/3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\( z \\) are:\n\\[\nz = \\pm e^{i\\pi/6} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad z = \\pm e^{-i\\pi/6}\n\\]\n\nThese solutions can be written in the form of \\( n \\)-th roots of unity. Specifically, the roots are:\n\\[\nz = e^{i\\pi/6}, \\quad z = e^{-i\\pi/6}, \\quad z = e^{i5\\pi/6}, \\quad z = e^{-i5\\pi/6}\n\\]\n\nThese roots are 12th roots of unity because:\n\\[\n(e^{i\\pi/6})^{12} = e^{i2\\pi} = 1, \\quad (e^{-i\\pi/6})^{12} = e^{-i2\\pi} = 1, \\quad (e^{i5\\pi/6})^{12} = e^{i10\\pi} = 1, \\quad (e^{-i5\\pi/6})^{12} = e^{-i10\\pi} = 1\n\\]\n\nThe smallest \\( n \\) such that all these roots are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity is the least common multiple (LCM) of the denominators of the arguments when expressed in radians. The arguments are \\( \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\), \\( -\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\), \\( \\frac{5\\pi}{6} \\), and \\( -\\frac{5\\pi}{6} \\). The denominators are 6, and the LCM of 6 is 6.\n\nHowever, we need to check if a smaller \\( n \\) might work. Since the roots are already 12th roots of unity, the smallest \\( n \\) is indeed 12.\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]",
